WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:03.480
Mind Your Decisions, I'm Presh Talwalkar

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:10.889
Find the coefficient of x to the power of nine in the expansion of the following binomial product

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.440
The first term is 1 plus x

00:00:14.019 --> 00:00:16.019
multiplied by 1 plus x squared

00:00:16.029 --> 00:00:22.139
Multiplied by 1 plus x cubed all the way until the last term of 1 plus x to the power of 100

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:31.000
This was asked on the Jee Advanced exam which is a test used for admission into India's prestigious IIT colleges

00:00:31.719 --> 00:00:37.469
Pause the video if you'd like to give it a try. And when you're ready keep watching to learn how to solve this problem.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.410
Let's analyze this binomial product

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:56.220
Consider the term 1 plus x to the power of 10

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:03.450
What happens if we multiply x to the power of 10 by ones in all of the other binomial terms?

00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:06.479
We'll end up with x to the power of 10

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:10.919
Notice the exponent is larger than x to the power of 9

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:15.149
So this will not contribute to the coefficient of x to the power of 9

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:23.729
similarly, if we have 1 plus x to the power of k where k is greater than 9. If we multiply this by all of

00:01:23.729 --> 00:01:28.019
the 1 terms in all of the other binomials we'll end up with a term

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:31.079
that is an exponent that's larger than x to the power of 9

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:36.940
So we don't need to focus on any of these terms to get the coefficient of x to the power of 9

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:44.960
We'll only go up to 1 plus x to the power of 9 and then we'll distribute this term with all the other ones in all of the other binomials.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:47.760
So let's analyze this binomial product

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:50.160
We will recall an exponent rule

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:55.140
x to the power of A multiplied by x to the power B is equal to x to the power of A plus B

00:01:56.289 --> 00:02:02.819
We are therefore going to count the ways that the exponents sum to 9 and the exponents are the numbers from 1 to 9

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:08.490
We'll then multiply the result by ones from all of the other binomials in the product

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:15.839
Now here's a simple observation: 1+2+3+4 = 10 and that's greater than 9

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.130
So we can only have 3, 2, or 1 terms in the product

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:23.609
If we have 4 terms, we're gonna get to an exponent that's larger than 9

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:25.900
So let's enumerate the ways

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.720
If we have one term, we just have x to the power of 9

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:35.760
For two terms we can have 8 + 1. We can have 7 + 2. We can have 6 + 3,  or we can have 5 + 4.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:38.020
What about three terms?

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.280
One way is 6 + 2 + 1

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:45.500
Another way is 5 + 3 + 1. And a final way is 4 + 3 + 2

00:02:45.819 --> 00:02:48.179
There's no other way to get to a power of 9

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:57.480
Interestingly the problem relates to number theory because these are the partitions of the number 9 from the set 1 through 9.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:02.700
We count these and there are a total of 8 ways. And that's the answer.

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:08.660
Just for fun we can input this into Wolfram Alpha and it will verify that the answer is 8

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:11.299
Incredible

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:15.220
Thanks for making Mind Your Decisions one of the best channels on YouTube

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:19.620
As always, thanks for watching and thanks for your support

